1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,167
Students learn equations as if they work everywhere.

2
00:00:03,167 --> 00:00:04,750
Scientific models are approximations.

3
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:09,500
Understanding where they stop matching reality is part of understanding
the science.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,499
ModelScope makes those limits visible across mechanics,

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,054
physics approximations,

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,349
chemistry,

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,799
and instrumentation.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,121
Here, ModelScope tests Hooke’s law against synthetic spring
measurements.

9
00:00:29,121 --> 00:00:35,532
It fits the configured baseline, then compares continuous segmented
alternatives on the same observations.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,698
Each finding links back to its measurements.

11
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:44,323
Students can inspect and edit the source values, then rerun the
analysis.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,475
A better segmented fit alone is not enough.

13
00:00:48,475 --> 00:00:52,818
ModelScope penalizes additional complexity and the search across
candidate transitions.

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,684
It requires sustained, same-direction residual disagreement.

15
00:00:56,684 --> 00:01:02,500
An influence safeguard checks that one unusual point does not
automatically create support.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,740
Here, the supported transition is near zero point zero eight meters.

17
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:11,500
Its sensitivity range is not a confidence interval or proof of a
physical mechanism.

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,874
The same framework tests the small-angle pendulum approximation,

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,024
Beer–Lambert calibration,

20
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,174
and sensor linearity, with model-specific assumptions and units.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,304
Students can bring CSV, pasted tables, or manual measurements.

22
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,989
They map variables, choose a supported model, declare units, and provide
a justified measurement response scale.

23
00:01:40,989 --> 00:01:43,173
No uncertainty is silently invented.

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,574
Their data runs through the same deterministic evidence pipeline.

25
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,603
Gemini translates the experiment description into a constrained setup
proposal.

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,619
The student reviews and confirms it.

27
00:02:01,619 --> 00:02:04,238
The response scale remains their responsibility.

28
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,028
Gemini explains existing evidence without choosing breakpoints or
changing support states.

29
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,303
AI explains.

30
00:02:13,303 --> 00:02:15,909
The analysis engine decides.

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,824
Raw measurements stay in the browser for normal analysis.

32
00:02:21,824 --> 00:02:27,773
Optional AI receives only description and headers, or a selected finding
and summary context.

33
00:02:27,773 --> 00:02:29,047
Avoid sensitive context.

34
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,150
ModelScope helps students learn not only how equations work, but when
those equations deserve to be trusted.

35
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:41,412
Equations have limits.

36
00:02:41,412 --> 00:02:42,253
Find them.
